{
  "dom_id": "step-q35906",
  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "The projection of the intersection between the hyperbolic paraboloid $\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=z+2$ and the plane \n$y=3$  onto the $xz$-plane is a parabola. What is the vertex of this parabola?",
  "worked_solution": "To find the intersection of the paraboloid and the plane $y=3$, we substitute $y=3$ into the equation of the paraboloid, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =z+2 \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{3}^{2}}{9} & =z+2 \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}-1 & =z+2 \\\\ z+3 & =\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4} \\\\ z & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+1)^{2}-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of a parabola in the $xz$-plane that has its vertex at $(-1,-3).$",
